[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(LaFosse et al., 2023-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/26ba0364095470a0f3fb647cfdb2aa7a50e25d8a)
- venue: eNeuro
- citation count: 11
[method]
The paper used a bicistronic AAV vector expressing jGCaMP8s-P2A-stChrimsonR in mouse primary visual cortex to enable simultaneous two-photon calcium imaging and two-photon holographic photostimulation of the same neurons. The approach combined in vivo two-photon calcium imaging to record spontaneous and visually evoked activity with holographic photostimulation to drive selected cells, while visual stimulation and retinotopic mapping were used to characterize sensory responses. The authors also performed visual tuning analysis and repeated-session stability analyses, including measurement of photostimulation response half-decay times. This is directly related to the proposed AAV9-hSyn-GCaMP8s-T2A-rsChRmine method because both use a bicistronic construct to co-express a green calcium indicator and a red-shifted opsin for simultaneous readout and optogenetic manipulation of the same neurons. The key difference is that the paper validates the strategy in V1 with visual stimulation and stChrimsonR, whereas the proposed plan applies a similar strategy to auditory cortex with sound stimulation and rsChRmine.
[results]
The reported outcomes were that the bicistronic construct produced robust expression of both jGCaMP8s and stChrimsonR, that V1 cells expressing the construct showed visually evoked and spontaneous calcium signals, that visual responses were stable over time, and that holographic photostimulation evoked responses in expressing cells. The paper further reported that both optogenetic and visual responses were robust and stable across sessions. The supplied excerpt does not provide numeric values such as cell counts, response amplitudes, correlation coefficients, half-decay times, or statistical significance values, so no quantitative outcomes can be extracted from the provided text.
[context]
The experimental context was in vivo mouse V1, with two-photon imaging and holographic photostimulation of the same neurons, visual stimulation, and repeated sessions to assess stability of sensory and optogenetic responses. The proposed research plan adapts this simultaneous imaging and manipulation framework to awake head-fixed mouse primary auditory cortex layer 2/3, targeting small ensembles of frequency-tuned neurons at 16 or 54 kHz and using a 0 mW control to separate optogenetic effects from natural adaptation. The paper supports the feasibility of the proposed bicistronic GCaMP8s-red opsin approach for simultaneous readout and manipulation, but it does not test the auditory co-tuned rebalancing hypothesis or provide direct evidence for frequency-specific ensemble interactions.
[end related work 2]

