- venue: bioRxiv
- citation count: 0
[method]
The paper used an all-optical in vivo approach in mouse primary visual cortex: a bicistronic construct co-expressing jGCaMP8s and stChrimsonR was expressed in V1 cells; widefield fluorescence confirmed expression; two-photon calcium imaging recorded visually evoked and spontaneous activity; retinotopic mapping and visual stimulation characterized visual responses; two-photon holographic photostimulation activated targeted cells while the broader field of view was imaged; and analyses quantified visual tuning and the half-decay time of photostimulation-evoked calcium responses. This is methodologically related to the proposed plan because it demonstrates simultaneous two-photon calcium imaging and holographic optogenetic stimulation in a sensory cortex, which is the core technique for targeting small ensembles while monitoring the field of view. The paper, however, used visual V1 cells rather than auditory cortex frequency-tuned ensembles and did not report the proposed 16/54 kHz pure-tone or 0 mW control design.
[results]
The reported outcomes were qualitative in the supplied excerpt: jGCaMP8s and stChrimsonR were robustly expressed from the bicistronic construct; visually evoked and spontaneous V1 responses were stable over time; holographic photostimulation produced reliable optogenetic responses; and visual tuning remained stable across sessions. The paper also measured a half-decay time for photostimulation responses, indicating that optogenetic activation was transient rather than persistently elevated. No numeric response amplitudes, tuning selectivity values, ensemble sizes, or statistical significance values are provided in the supplied excerpt, so quantitative comparisons to the proposed auditory ensemble manipulation cannot be made.
[context]
Key conditions were mouse V1, bicistronic jGCaMP8s-P2A-stChrimsonR expression, in vivo two-photon calcium imaging, in vivo two-photon holographic photostimulation, visual stimulation with retinotopic mapping, and analysis of visual tuning and photostimulation response decay. These conditions support the feasibility of the proposed all-optical, single-cell-resolution stimulation/imaging paradigm and the need for session-to-session stability, but they do not establish auditory frequency tuning, 5-cell co-tuned ensemble targeting, pure-tone response stability, or the specific 100 ms/5 mW/15 μm spiral/30-revolution stimulation parameters used in the proposed plan.
[end related work 1]



