- in_text_citation: [(James et al., 2018-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e2a18feaf7a7bfd92f6159c891cca2567bcc4836)
- venue: bioRxiv
- citation count: 22
[method]
The closest related method in the paper was an optogenetic stimulation condition used to test whether light-driven cholinergic activation altered passive auditory responses. Awake head-fixed Chat-ChR2 mice received optogenetic stimulation while auditory evoked LFPs and multi-unit activity were recorded. For optogenetic analyses, LFPs were bandpass filtered at 1-150 Hz, downsampled to 381 Hz, and motion-contaminated trials were rejected using RMS criteria. Normalized spectrograms were computed by dividing power in each time window by the mean power in a 5-s baseline immediately before optogenetic stimulation. Cortical desynchrony was calculated as the ratio of 10-100 Hz power to 1-10 Hz power and compared during a 750-ms light stimulation window to the 750-ms period immediately before light onset. The paper also reports a sound-evoked amplitude comparison for optogenetic stimulation, but the supplied excerpt does not describe a delayed post-stimulation tone series; thus it provides a no-stimulation baseline for acute optogenetic effects rather than a direct test of persistence after a stimulation session.
[results]
Optogenetic stimulation significantly increased cortical desynchrony relative to the passive inter-trial interval baseline (Wilcoxon signed-rank test, p=6.036e-6). The paper reports that optogenetic stimulation did not alter sound-evoked amplitude, but no effect size or p-value is provided in the supplied excerpt. In the same passive-sound dataset, trial-to-trial cortical desynchrony during the inter-trial interval did not significantly predict PFC ERP magnitude (Wilcoxon rank-sum test, p=0.879), indicating that baseline desynchrony was not a major determinant of the passive auditory PFC response.
[context]
The study used 38 mice total: 32 Chat-ChR2 transgenic mice and 6 Ai32 control mice for optogenetic experiments. Recordings were made from awake, head-fixed mice with simultaneous PFC and AC multi-contact probes. The auditory stimulus was 70 dB SPL acoustic white noise, with a 10-s inter-trial interval; spike analyses used 500-ms sound windows. LFP processing used a 1-150 Hz bandpass filter, 381 Hz sampling, and desynchrony was defined as 10-100 Hz power divided by 1-10 Hz power. The optogenetic comparison used a 750-ms stimulation window versus the immediately preceding 750-ms baseline. This differs from the proposed plan, which uses 100-ms 16 or 54 kHz pure tones, 2-s or 5.8-6.5-s inter-stimulus intervals, two-photon GCaMP8s imaging, holographic rsChRmine stimulation of five co-tuned AC neurons, and a separate post-stimulation session without light to test whether changes persist.
[end related work 1]

