[method]
The paper used widefield and two-photon imaging analysis of mouse primary auditory cortex (A1) during acoustic stimulation to characterize frequency preference and local spatial tuning heterogeneity. The approach compared F1 (CBAxC57) and C57BL/6 mice, using imaging-derived frequency preference maps to assess how neighboring neurons differ in their preferred frequencies and how this heterogeneity changes across spatial scales. This relates to the proposed research method because it uses two-photon imaging to examine the spatial distribution of frequency-tuned neurons, which is conceptually aligned with the plan’s goal of mapping co-tuned ensembles and assessing the spatial distribution of affected neurons. However, the paper does not use holographic optogenetic stimulation and does not directly test the temporal dynamics of stimulation-induced rebalancing.
[results]
F1 (CBAxC57) A1 neurons showed a broader range of frequency preference than C57BL/6 mice. F1 (CBAxC57) mice had higher local heterogeneity of frequency preference than C57BL/6 mice. The increased tuning heterogeneity in F1 mice was reported to be present at multiple length scales. Signal correlations were higher in C57BL/6 mice than in F1 mice. The supplied excerpt does not provide numeric effect sizes, sample sizes, or statistical significance values.
