[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Ronzitti et al., 2017-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/20b6db4822512484b3fda84d998d801e21196604)
- venue: No Venue
- citation count: 104
[method]
The paper describes two-photon optogenetic stimulation using parallel light-targeting, especially computer-generated holography (CGH) and temporal focusing (TF), to project diffraction-limited spots or extended patterns into tissue. A phase-only liquid-crystal spatial light modulator is placed at the back focal plane of the objective, and holographic algorithms such as prisms-and-lenses, Gerchberg-Saxton, and multi-plane Gerchberg-Saxton variants calculate the phase mask needed to produce the desired intensity distribution at the sample. Temporal focusing with a diffraction grating confines excitation axially and improves robustness to scattering. In multi-plane implementations, two spatial light modulators are used: one shapes the lateral pattern and the second applies a spherical phase to shift the pattern to a chosen axial plane, with tiling of the modulators to address several planes. The paper does not explicitly report the proposed distance-decay control, but it demonstrates the spatial confinement needed for that control. The proposed method can be implemented by shifting the holographic stimulation pattern, such as the 15 μm spiral pattern in the research plan, laterally or axially relative to selected auditory-cortex neurons and measuring whether the optogenetic response decreases with distance from the intended target cells.
[results]
The paper reports that temporal focusing combined with GPC activated ChR2-expressing layer V cortical neurons in acute mouse brain slices at low excitation intensities of 0.3-0.6 mW/μm^2 and selectively activated subcellular structures such as basal and apical dendrites while preserving lateral and axial resolution. Temporally focused CGH activated C1V1-expressing neurons in brain slices with 1 ms illumination at 0.5 mW/μm^2, producing spiking. Using a high-peak-power ytterbium laser with 20 μJ pulses, two-photon CGH activated Chronos, CoChR, and ReaChR in cell cultures and acute mouse visual cortex slices with millisecond temporal resolution and sub-millisecond timing accuracy. For Chronos, with an off-time constant of about 3.8 ms, reliable 100 Hz light-driven firing was achieved with 2-3 ms illumination, average power of 0.05-0.17 mW/μm^2, and jitter per action potential of about 0.2-1 ms over ten consecutive spikes, with no extra spikes and a small plateau potential. For scattering robustness, GPC or CGH combined with temporal focusing maintained activation at penetration depths of at least 500 μm. ChR2-expressing molecular layer interneurons in vitro were activated with 15 μm diameter spots at an average depth of 201 ± 9 μm, producing average inward currents of about 120 pA during 10 ms illumination, and activation was spatially restricted to the pattern. In zebrafish brain, temporally focused holographic beams were effective at about 500 μm depth. Multi-plane CGH projected extended patterns in a 240 × 240 × 260 μm^3 volume and allowed remote axial displacement of a single plane over about 300 μm or independent patterns on several axial planes.
[context]
The relevant experiments were performed in acute mouse brain slices, opsin-expressing cell cultures, and zebrafish brain, using opsins such as ChR2, C1V1, Chronos, CoChR, and ReaChR. The optical system used phase-only liquid-crystal spatial light modulators for CGH, a diffraction grating for temporal focusing, and in some designs a second spatial light modulator for axial control. Illumination was two-photon, with either Ti:sapphire oscillators or high-peak-power ytterbium amplified pulses. Typical stimulation parameters included 1-10 ms illumination durations, spot diameters around 15 μm, low average powers of 0.05-0.6 mW/μm^2, and depths from about 200 μm to 500 μm. Important constraints included two-photon speckle inhomogeneities, roughly 20% for one-photon and 50% for two-photon CGH, which can be reduced by time averaging or speckle-free algorithms; underfilled pupil reduces effective numerical aperture and lateral resolution; and spatial light modulator tiling limits the number of independently addressed axial planes. These conditions support the proposed awake mouse auditory-cortex plan, which uses simultaneous two-photon GCaMP8s imaging and holographic rsChRmine stimulation of five cells with a 15 μm spiral pattern at 5 mW per cell for 100 ms. The paper's evidence of spatially restricted, low-power two-photon holographic activation provides the physical basis for the proposed control in which stimulation position is varied relative to target cells and response decay with distance is measured.
[end related work 1]

