[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Triplett et al., 2023-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/9d1fdb72be2328293d106365d9f20374230ad1a3)
- venue: bioRxiv
- citation count: 7
[method]
The paper used a spatially explicit optogenetic receptive-field (ORF) mapping and Bayesian target-optimization method to assess how evoked response depends on stimulation position relative to target cells. In each simulated neuron, holographic stimulation was delivered at a grid of x/y offsets around the cell and at multiple laser powers, and the resulting activation responses were fit with a GP-Bernoulli model to estimate the probability of activation as a function of distance, direction, depth, and power. The fitted ORFs were then used in a projected gradient descent algorithm to select hologram positions and laser powers that maximized the intended target's predicted activation while minimizing predicted off-target activation, subject to an upper bound on laser power. The gradient of the objective function was computed by exploiting the joint Gaussian process distribution of the ORF and its spatial derivative, and the optimizer was run from multiple random initializations near the target soma. This is directly related to the proposed control experiment because it varies stimulation position relative to target cells and measures response decay with distance; however, the paper goes beyond a simple decay curve by modeling the full spatial response field and using it to reposition holograms to reduce off-target stimulation in the proposed all-optical ensemble-stimulation plan.
[results]
In two-dimensional synthetic optogenetics experiments containing 116 putative opsin-expressing neurons, single-target hologram optimization reduced average write-in error by 85% compared with high-power nuclear stimulation. The average displacement between the original nuclear stimulus and the optimized stimulus was 4.9 µm. In three-dimensional experiments that also allowed depth adjustment, optimization reduced average optical write-in error by 89%, from 0.7 to 0.08, with an average displacement of 8.3 µm. Some spatially isolated neurons required no repositioning, whereas densely packed neurons required both repositioning and laser-power adjustment to eliminate off-target stimulation. No statistical significance tests or confidence intervals are reported in the supplied sections. These results support the proposed plan's validation strategy by showing that position-dependent response measurements can be used to quantify and reduce off-target activation, although the paper's evidence is synthetic/slice-derived rather than in vivo auditory-cortex control data.
[context]
The paper's method was applied to synthetic experiments built from four detailed cell-attached recordings of opsin-expressing L2/3 interneurons in mouse V1 slice, with a separate fluorescence image used to place 116 putative opsin-expressing neurons in space. Each simulated neuron was assigned one of four ground-truth ORFs. ORF mapping used a spatial grid from -20 to 20 µm in 10 µm steps in x and y, with laser powers of 30, 50, and 70 mW for general simulations; the slice-based synthetic experiments used 10, 25, and 40 mW to match the underlying slice power range. Three-dimensional simulations added depths from -60 to 60 µm in 30 µm steps. GP-Bernoulli kernel hyperparameters were selected by 5-fold cross-validation over radial lengthscales 2, 4, 8, and 16, power lengthscales 2, 4, 8, and 16, and amplitudes 1, 2, 4, 8, and 16; the selected values were radial lengthscale 8, power lengthscale 16, and amplitude 8. Optimization used a laser-power upper bound and multiple random initializations. The proposed research plan differs in that it uses awake mouse auditory cortex layer 2/3, GCaMP8s/rsChRmine co-expression, five-cell co-tuned ensembles, 5 mW per cell, and a 15 µm spiral stimulation pattern; the paper therefore provides a related computational and synthetic validation framework for position-response controls rather than a direct in vivo demonstration under the proposed plan's conditions.
[end related work 2]

