[method]
The paper used a holographic optogenetic stimulation validation in human hiPSC-derived neuronal networks on MEA chips. Holographic light patterns were projected to target a single opsin-expressing neuron, and MEA recordings were analyzed for AP frequency and burst frequency in the stimulated electrode and neighboring electrodes. The stimulation window was compared with a pre-stimulation baseline window, and successful activation was inferred from an evoked increase in AP or burst frequency above baseline. The proportion of trials in which the targeted neuron met this activation criterion was assessed, and evoked responses in neighboring electrodes were binned by physical distance from the stimulated site. This is directly analogous to the proposed plan's validation of five-cell ensemble activation by measuring the proportion of target neurons activated across trials and by measuring response decay with distance, although the paper's implementation was single-neuron, in vitro, and MEA-based rather than multi-cell, in vivo, and calcium-imaging-based.
